Overflow Faecal Incontinence
Geriatric & Frailty
Involuntary stool passage, Decreased anal sphincter tone, Fecal impaction with overflow

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-025
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of unmanageable overflow incontinence causing severe perianal skin excoriation and imminent breakdown of home care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage confirmed acute, painful perianal skin breakdown and continuous overflow diarrhea requiring urgent physical examination and disimpaction. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of progressive tissue necrosis, secondary infection, and total caregiver crisis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is constant liquid stool leaking, the skin is completely raw, and they cry out in pain during pad changes."
History of Present Illness: Condition evolved over 72 hours, initially presenting as reduced bowel frequency and abdominal discomfort. Over the past 24 hours, this progressed to continuous involuntary loss of liquid or solid stool. Patient exhibits an inability to delay defecation until an appropriate social context, leading to persistent soiling of undergarments requiring frequent changes or use of incontinence pads. The situation is compounded by the presence of a functionally limiting cognitive or mobility impairment precluding timely toilet access.
Functional Status: Bedbound
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent caregiver burnout, high risk of compromised hygiene, and impaired skin integrity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Frail, actively distressed during physical repositioning.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: mildly distended, non-tender, active bowel sounds. Perianal: erythematous excoriation extending to gluteal cleft. Examination confirms involuntary stool passage, decreased anal sphincter tone, and palpable rectal fecal impaction with overflow liquid stool. Digital rectal examination also reveals reduced voluntary squeeze pressure of the external anal sphincter, altered perianal sensation to pinprick or light touch, and loss of the anocutaneous reflex. Expected investigation results: evidence of external or internal anal sphincter defects on endoanal ultrasound that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is adequately heated. High volume of soiled incontinence pads in waste receptacle confirms excessive output. Trip hazards removed from bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is taking regular opioid analgesia without concurrent prophylactic aperients.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Faecal Incontinence - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Infectious Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of prominent fever, systemic inflammatory signs, or hyperactive bowel sounds.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: lack of gross hematochezia and no previous history of relapsing inflammatory abdominal pain.

Colorectal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of a palpable mass distinct from fecal matter and no reported chronic unintentional weight loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically indicated to manage acute overflow fecal incontinence secondary to severe impaction. Telephone triage identified rapid-onset perianal skin breakdown and significant distress, necessitating immediate intervention. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of progressive tissue necrosis, secondary bacterial cellulitis, and the total breakdown of the home care environment due to caregiver inability to manage the continuous output. Immediate manual disimpaction and implementation of a skin management protocol were required to stabilize the patient's acute physical decline and prevent an avoidable emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Manual disimpaction performed. Prescribed topical zinc oxide barrier cream. Initiated daily osmotic laxative regimen. Recommended formal endoanal ultrasound via regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To encourage necessary fluid intake, the caregiver will offer 100ml of water immediately following each care task; successful intake will be immediately paired with preferred quiet music or verbal praise to reinforce the hydration behavior.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing for ongoing wound care and continence nurse specialist.
DME/Safety (Optional): High-absorbency, breathable continence aids and a pressure-relieving mattress overlay.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to reassess bowel habit and monitor skin integrity.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a core temperature above 38.0 C, new onset confusion, severe generalized abdominal pain, rigors, or absolute cessation of flatus and stool passage despite interventions.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expected gradual reduction of overflow leakage over 48 hours following disimpaction. Skin healing is anticipated to take up to seven days with consistent barrier cream application.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if redness spreads rapidly beyond the buttocks, if high fever develops, or if the patient becomes completely unable to pass urine or stool within the next 12 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Manual removal of the blockage allows the bowel to empty normally, which stops the continuous leak of liquid stool around the hard mass. The barrier cream protects the raw skin from further acid damage and promotes healing.
Actionable Steps: Offer small, manageable sips of water linked to scheduled turning intervals to successfully rebuild hydration habits without overwhelming the patient.

